/tmp/tmp.5t1LnOgBFE/src/main.tex:116:\section{NP-Completeness}\label{sec:NPC}
/tmp/tmp.5t1LnOgBFE/src/main.tex:118:Let {\sc $\colo{a}{b}$-coloring} be the problem of partitioning the vertex set of a graph into $a$ independent sets and $b$ $2$-independent sets. In this section, we will prove hardness results for some variants of {\sc$\colo{a}{b}$-coloring}. The map associating a color to each vertex has polynomial size, and the verification procedure is a simple algorithm that checks that every vertex is well colored, thus it has polynomial time complexity. Therefore, for every integer $a$ and $b$, {\sc$\colo{a}{b}$-coloring} is in NP ($a$ and $b$ being part of the input or not). So, in what follows, in every NP-Completeness proof, it is sufficient to prove NP-hardness. 
