237-<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
238:<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chromatic number</em> of a graph <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the least integer <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that the vertex set of <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned into <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> independent sets. The problem of computing the chromatic number of a graph is one of the oldest and most widely studied problems in graph theory, from both an algorithmic and from a structural point of view. Those two points of views lead to very famous and celebrated results, such as the NP-Completeness of <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> even when <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> and in planar graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, or the Four Color Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> stating that the chromatic number of every planar graph is at most <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the only known proofs of the latter result are computer-assisted due to the number of cases to study, and no combinatorial proof of the Four Color Theorem verifiable without computer assistance is known. In this direction, there has been recent interest regarding variants of the Four Color Theorem, and more precisely, strengthenings of the Five Color Theorem. For instance, it is proven in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> that it is possible to <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-color any planar graph of order <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> such that at least one color class is of size at most <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/6</annotation></semantics></math>, which is better than the <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/5</annotation></semantics></math> bound provided by the pigeon hole principle. Although this statement is much weaker than the Four Color Theorem, the proof is not computer-assisted. The Four Color Theorem also provides the best possible lower-bound for the size of the largest independent set in a planar graph <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)\geq n/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)\geq n/4</annotation></semantics></math> which is attained for a disjoint union of <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is no known proof of this bound that is independent of the Four Color Theorem, and the best known lower-bound independent of the Four Color Theorem is <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)\geq 3n/13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)\geq 3n/13</annotation></semantics></math> provided in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
239-</div>
240-<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
241-<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A variant of the chromatic number is the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-distance chromatic number</em>, denoted <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{2}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{2}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, introduced by Kramer and Kramer in 1969 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and defined as the least integer <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that the vertex set of <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned into <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-independent sets. The decision problem asking, for a graph <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, whether or not <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{2}(G)\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{2}(G)\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> is called <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-distance <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring</span> and it was proven NP-complete for every <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. In fact, a much more general result is provided in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> regarding <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-distance <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring</span>, which is NP-Complete if <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;\lfloor 3d/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;\lfloor 3d/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and Polynomial if <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq\lfloor 3d/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq\lfloor 3d/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. A conjecture of Wegner from 1977 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> about the value of <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in planar graphs has generated substantial interest:</p>
