596-<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
597-<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We reduce <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">restricted planar 3-sat</span>, which is known to be NP-Complete by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 2 NP-Completeness ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Let <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> be an instance of <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">restricted planar 3-sat</span> and let <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> be the planar variable-clause incidence graph of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, for every variable <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a vertex <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for every clause <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a vertex <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, for every clause <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and variable <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> has the edge <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}v_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}v_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
598:Let <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph such that there exists a <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the vertices of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> colored with a distance-1 color induce a graph from <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that there exists a vertex <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in V(H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in V(H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g})</annotation></semantics></math>, for every <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is colored with a distance-2 color.
599:We construct from <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> the graph <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as follows :</span></p>
600-<ul id="S2.I10" class="ltx_itemize">
601-<li id="S2.I10.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
602-<div id="S2.I10.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
603-<p id="S2.I10.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every variable <math id="S2.I10.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.I10.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, we add a copy of the graph <math id="S2.I10.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{VAR}_{x}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>VAR</mtext><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{VAR}_{x}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.4 (1^2,2^𝑘)-coloring in planar graphs ‣ 2 NP-Completeness ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and</p>
604-</div></li>
--
606-<div id="S2.I10.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
607-<p id="S2.I10.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every clause <math id="S2.I10.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with variables <math id="S2.I10.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I10.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I10.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, we add the gadget <math id="S2.I10.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}_{C}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}_{C}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.4 (1^2,2^𝑘)-coloring in planar graphs ‣ 2 NP-Completeness ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and</p>
608-</div></li>
609-<li id="S2.I10.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
610-<div id="S2.I10.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
611:<p id="S2.I10.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every clause containing only two literals, say <math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, we add a copy of <math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}_{C}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}_{C}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.4 (1^2,2^𝑘)-coloring in planar graphs ‣ 2 NP-Completeness ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, a copy of <math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we connect with an edge <math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{z}</annotation></semantics></math> to the vertex <math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> from the copy of <math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
612-</div></li>
613-<li id="S2.I10.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
614-<div id="S2.I10.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
615-<p id="S2.I10.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, for every clause <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and every vertex <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> appears positively in <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> then we connect the vertex <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to the vertex <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{C}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>C</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{C}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with an edge, otherwise we connect the vertex <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to the vertex <math id="S2.I10.i4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{C}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>C</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{C}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with an edge.</p>
616-</div></li>
617-</ul>
618:<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that since <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>VAR</mtext><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> are planar, and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the variable-clause incidence graph of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, an instance of <span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">restricted planar 3-sat</span>, then <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a planar graph. By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Choi and Liu [], 2025). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, every planar graph of girth at least 10 is <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable, so we can assume that <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> and so <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>VAR</mtext><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> are allowed to have a cycle of length <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is of girth at least <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> as well. Also, observe that both <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>VAR</mtext><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable, and, in any <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>VAR</mtext><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is colored with a distance-1 color if and only if <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is colored with a distance-2 color so we can <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-color <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>VAR</mtext><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{VAR}^{k}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the vertices colored with a distance-1 color induce a <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{8}</annotation></semantics></math>.
619-Similarly, in any <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, at least one of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{y}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is colored with a distance-2 color so we can <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-color <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the vertices colored with a distance-1 color induce a disjoint union of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m62" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{8}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
620-<ul id="S2.I11" class="ltx_itemize">
621-<li id="S2.I11.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
622-<div id="S2.I11.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
623:<p id="S2.I11.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfiable, then let us fix a satisfiable truth assignment to the variables of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. For every variable <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned true, then we color <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with a distance-1 color, and <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with a distance-2 color. Otherwise, we color <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with a distance-2 color and <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with a distance-1 color. Then, we can easily extend this partial coloring in order to color every copy of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{VAR}_{g}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>VAR</mtext><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{VAR}_{g}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by coloring the uncolored vertices from the cycle of length <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> by alternating between the two distance-1 colors, hence obtaining a <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> colored with the two distance-1 colors, and then by coloring the copies of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the vertices colored with a distance-1 color induce a graph from <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, since every clause is satisfied, every clause must have a literal whose value is true. If this literal is <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, then the vertex <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected by an edge to <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> which is itself already colored with a distance-1 color. Therefore, there is an available distance-2 color to color <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, if the literal is <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg x</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected by an edge to <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> which is itself already colored with a distance-1 color. Therefore, there is an available distance-2 color to color <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. So in every copy of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, at least one of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{y}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is colored with a distance-2 color, and so we can easily extend the current partial <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring in order to color every copy of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>CL</mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{CL}^{k}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> since the vertices from the cycle of length <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> that are not yet colored induce a disjoint union of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{8}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, for every clause containing only two literals, we need to color the copy of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathcal{C}}^{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> that is connected from <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and this is doable since one distance-2 color is available for <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable and the <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> distance-<math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> colors induce a disjoint union of <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> and graphs from <math id="S2.I11.i1.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
624-</div></li>
625-<li id="S2.I11.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
626-<div id="S2.I11.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
627-<p id="S2.I11.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable, then let us fix a <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for every variable <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to be true if and only if <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is colored with a distance-1 color. By a previous observation, for every clause <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and for any <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{2},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{2},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a variable <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is colored with a distance-2 color. If <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> appears positively in <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected with an edge to <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> in which case <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> must be colored with a distance-1 color, and so <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied. Otherwise, <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{C}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{C}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected with an edge to <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> must be colored with a distance-1 color. Therefore, <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> must be colored with a distance-2 color and <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned false. Since <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> appears negatively in <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied. Therefore,every clause of <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied, and so is <math id="S2.I11.i2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
628-</div></li>
--
774-</div>
775-</section>
776-<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
777-<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6 </span><math id="S2.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{3},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{3},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-coloring</span> in planar graphs</h3>
778-
779:<figure id="S2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.13037v1/gadget_H2k.svg" id="S2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/171;" width="238" height="171"></object>
780:<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S2.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The gadgets used in the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem15" title="Theorem 15. ‣ 2.6 (1^3,2^𝑘)-coloring in planar graphs ‣ 2 NP-Completeness ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>. In <math id="S2.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> both <math id="S2.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> must be colored with distance-1 colors.</span></figcaption>
781-</figure>
782-<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
783-<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
784-<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
785-<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{3},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{3},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-coloring</span> is NP-Complete even when restricted to planar graphs of maximum degree <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3k+4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3k+4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
--
787-</div>
788-<div id="S2.SS6.3" class="ltx_proof">
789-<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
790-<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
791-<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We reduce <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-coloring</span>, which is known to be NP-Complete even when restricted to planar graphs of maximum degree <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 2 NP-Completeness ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
792:Consider the graph <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from Figure <a href="#S2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 2.6 (1^3,2^𝑘)-coloring in planar graphs ‣ 2 NP-Completeness ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, then in any <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{3},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{3},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring, both <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> must be colored with a distance-1 color. Indeed, one can observe that any vertex in <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(u)-v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(u)-v</annotation></semantics></math> must be colored with a distance-2 color and <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|N(u)-v|=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|N(u)-v|=k</annotation></semantics></math>, so no distance-2 color is available for <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
793:Let <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a planar graph of maximum degree <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. We construct the graph <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> starting from a copy of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and, for every vertex <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, we add a copy of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we identify <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a planar graph of maximum degree <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3k+4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3k+4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
794-<ul id="S2.I15" class="ltx_itemize">
795-<li id="S2.I15.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
796-<div id="S2.I15.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
797-<p id="S2.I15.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I15.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.I15.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable, then for every vertex <math id="S2.I15.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, we color <math id="S2.I15.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I15.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same distance-1 color. By the previous observations, this pre-coloring of <math id="S2.I15.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended into a <math id="S2.I15.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{3},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{3},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring.</p>
798-</div></li>
--
923-</div>
924-<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
925-<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Bounds on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ex}^{1}_{\mathcal{G}}\hskip-3.0pt{}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ex</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ex}^{1}_{\mathcal{G}}\hskip-3.0pt{}</annotation></semantics></math></h4>
926-
927-<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
928:<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph from Figure <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 3.2.1 Bounds on ex^1_𝒢 ‣ 3.2 Extremal values for planar graphs ‣ 3 Extremal values for the required number of distance-2 colors ‣ Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> is of order <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4k</annotation></semantics></math> and is not <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{1},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{1},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable. Indeed, assume otherwise that this graph is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{1},2^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{1},2^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable. Since <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(s)=\deg(t)&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(s)=\deg(t)&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> must be colored with a distance-2 color. So at least <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> must be colored with a distance-1 color, and similarly for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, so there exists <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that the edge <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is monochromatic, which is a contradiction. Notice that the graph from Figure <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 3.2.1 Bounds on ex^1_𝒢 ‣ 3.2 Extremal values for planar graphs ‣ 3 Extremal values for the required number of distance-2 colors ‣ Be