"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal in the sense that if we consider subclasses of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we quickly obtain graphs that are <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{1},2^{o(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{1},2^{o(n)})</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable.</p>
