+# arXiv:2602.13037 — compact mixed-coloring certificates
+
+> **Current status:** the generator and local gates G1–G8 pass, but this result is **not yet shippable**. The required OpenRouter runs all returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded,” so G9(b) and the bare hardening verdict remain unmeasured. API errors were not counted as oracle failures.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B** — no-tool compression; an efficient exact algorithm is disclosed |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT (mixed graph coloring) |
+| Certificate form | exact symbolic affine rule |
+| Intuition | invariant: recognize three parallel affine fibers carrying the forced three-color phase |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | Section 2.6, Theorem 15, instantiated at `k=1` |
+
+## The problem
+
+The source is Thomas Delépine’s [*Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13037). A `(1^a,2^b)`-coloring partitions vertices into `a` ordinary independent sets and `b` sets in which equal-colored vertices must be at graph distance at least three.
+
+This family uses Theorem 15. The solver receives a coordinate-labelled planar base graph and an exact compact description of the theorem’s forcing gadget at every base vertex. It must return an affine finite-field rule `[a,b,d]` whose value is one of `0,1,2` at every base vertex and properly colors every base edge. The rule expands to a `(1^3,2^1)`-coloring of the complete target graph. `verify` materializes that target coloring and checks every edge and every relevant length-two path; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+The reduction is paper-licensed rather than merely convenient. The solver sees both the planar graph and the forcing gadgets used by Theorem 15. The extra finite-field coordinates are a compact language for the coloring map, not a replacement for graph verification.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Theorem 15 proves worst-case NP-completeness for `(1^3,2^k)`-coloring on planar graphs of maximum degree `3k+4`; at `k=1` the target degree bound is seven. That theorem does **not** make this inverse-generated distribution hard on Track A. Indeed, the generated base is the square of a path and has a forced period-three coloring phase.
+
+The disclosed reference algorithm reconstructs the path order, tries the six colorings of one triangle, interpolates an affine rule, and exactly scans the target. It is `O(n + log q)`. At the shipping preset (`n=2400`, `q=1009`) it succeeded on 8/8 seeds with 110,443–110,587 counted operations; the latest eight-seed panel took 0.551247 s. The compact intended route notices that the phase classes are three parallel affine fibers and uses at most 198 exact operations around an anchor triangle. The paper’s easy boundary cases in Section 2—`(1^1,2^1)` star forests and `(1^0,2^3)` disjoint paths/cycles—are not the selected regime.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+This is the complete `demo` instance for seed 7:
+
+```text
+Compact (1^3,2^1)-coloring certificate
+
+Vertices are 0-based; arithmetic is modulo q=11. The target is formed from
+the base below. For every base vertex v add u(v), x(v), and t(v,j,r) for
+j in {0,1}, r in {0,1,2}. Add v--u(v), u(v)--x(v), all x(v)--t(v,j,r),
+and all three edges within each triple t(v,j,0..2). Each triple plus x(v)
+is K4. There are no other vertices or edges. The target has 54 vertices and
+93 edges.
+
+The answer [a,b,d] defines C(v)=(a*X(v)+b*Y(v)+d) mod 11. It must map every
+base vertex into {0,1,2} and give different values on every base edge. Then
+v receives D[C(v)], u(v) receives D[(C(v)+1) mod 3], x(v) receives S, and
+t(v,j,r) receives D[r].
+
+Base vertices:
+0: 5 7
+1: 10 10
+2: 7 4
+3: 1 8
+4: 9 2
+5: 6 7
+
+Base edges:
+2-4 3-4 0-5 2-3 1-3 0-2 1-2 4-5 0-4
+
+Anchor triangle: 4 5 0
+Probe vertices: 4 5 1
+
+Output: <answer>[a,b,d]</answer>, with 0<=a,b,d<11 and (a,b)!=(0,0).
+```
+
+The planted answer is `<answer>[9,3,2]</answer>` and `verify(inst, [9,3,2])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Dropping the last coefficient returns `(False, "expected 3 coefficients, got 2")`. This six-vertex base is genuinely hand-scale: one can solve three modular linear equations and check nine base edges on paper.
+
+## Difficulty
+
+| preset | base `n` | prime `q` | target vertices | target edges | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 6 | 11 | 54 | 93 | hand example; exact count available |
+| easy | 90 | 101 | 810 | 1,437 | local gates pass; oracle unavailable |
+| medium | 600 | 503 | 5,400 | 9,597 | local gates pass; oracle unavailable |
+| hard | 2,400 | 1,009 | 21,600 | 38,397 | intended shipping preset; oracle unavailable |
+
+`escalate` doubles `n` and raises `q` while the certificate remains exactly three integers. G7 verified `n=4800`, `q=10007` with the same three-atom answer.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 preset–seed witnesses verified and JSON-round-tripped |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | fenced, prose-wrapped tagged answer round-tripped |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structured affine guesses; language size 1,027,242,720 |
+| G5 | pass | shipping density 0/200,000; demo has exactly 2/1,320 valid rules; reference cost 110,521 operations |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n`, fixed three-atom witness, verification `ok` |
+| G8 | pass | 20/20 relabel/affine invariance, 20/20 carried witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | **pending/fail** | size caps pass (12 chars, 3 tokens, 3 atoms, 198 operations); hinted oracle gate has no completed attempt |
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+The harness-owned bare transcript contains only API errors, so none of these rows is evidence that a model failed the problem.
+
+| preset | seed | model | scored result | reason |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 1091855230 | GPT-5.6 Terra | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
+| easy | 1677427125 | Claude Sonnet 5 | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
+| easy | 2016487830 | Claude Sonnet 5 | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
+| easy | 721687519 | GPT-5.6 Terra | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
+
+No `hardened`, `too_easy`, or `cap_bound` verdict was produced. The loop must be rerun with a funded OpenRouter key before submission.
+
+## G9 arms
+
+| arm | solved / completed attempts | status |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | four API errors; unmeasured |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | four API errors; unmeasured |
+| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | four API errors; unmeasured |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo statistic is therefore undefined in substance (stored as `0.0` only because both denominators are zero). No conclusion about the usefulness of the affine-fiber hint is justified. The size/effort half of G9 is healthy: 12 serialized characters, three atomic elements, approximately three tokens, and 198 intended exact operations.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2602_13037 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=42, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+prompt = render(inst)
+candidate = parse_answer("<answer>[1,2,3]</answer>")
+ok, reason = verify(inst, candidate)
+```
+
+From the repository root, after the oracle gate has actually passed:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2602.13037 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is deliberately a Track B benchmark. The base graph is a path square, so a tool can recover its three-color phase in linear time. Worst-case NP-completeness is context, not a claim about this distribution.
+- The 0/200,000 density estimate samples uniformly from nonzero-linear affine rules, the exact declared certificate language. It does not estimate the density of arbitrary vertex colorings, and zero observed hits is not proof that the true probability is zero.
+- The generator filters presentations defeated by its named greedy, extreme-coordinate, swapped-coefficient, and small-axis probes. This is construction-aware filtering, not a proof against every heuristic.
+- The tested attacks do not include an external SAT/SMT solver or a full general-purpose planar-coloring implementation. The successful specialized reference algorithm is stronger for this distribution but is not evidence of worst-case hardness.
+- `canonical_key` is complete for the generated path-square order up to the tested relabeling, reversal, and invertible affine coordinate transformations; it is not a general planar-graph canonical-labeling algorithm.
+- Most importantly, the multi-vendor oracle and G9(b) checks are absent because of account quota, not because the models failed. Do not trust or emit this family as hardened until those script-owned runs complete.
diff --git a/REJECTED.md b/REJECTED.md
deleted file mode 100644
index 0688218646a05bac3925728f98cc1ced60c7ec0d..0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
--- a/REJECTED.md
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,43 +0,0 @@
-# Rejected: arXiv 2602.13037
-
-[*Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13037) was read in full.  The proposed generator is rejected because **H fails on both tracks** (and the concrete Track-B prototype also fails G4, G5, G6, and G9(c)).  G and V are sound: the generator samples an affine coloring before constructing the graph, carries it through the paper's gadget, and the checker expands the target graph and scans every edge and length-two path exactly.
-
-## Paper result and regime
-
-Section 1 defines a `(1^a,2^b)`-coloring as a partition into `a` independent sets and `b` sets whose distinct vertices are at graph distance at least three.  Section 2 lists the polynomial boundary cases `(1^2,2^0)`, `(1^1,2^1)`, `(1^0,2^2)`, and `(1^0,2^3)`.  Theorem 6 proves NP-completeness for `(1^1,2^3)` on bipartite planar graphs of maximum degree four and arbitrary prescribed girth.  The retained prototype instead instantiates Section 2.6, Theorem 15 at `k=1`: attach `H_1^1` at every vertex of a planar maximum-degree-four graph, carrying a proper 3-coloring to a `(1^3,2^1)`-coloring of maximum degree seven.
-
-## Why Track A fails
-
-Theorems 6 and 15 are worst-case reductions.  They do not establish hardness for an inverse-generated distribution.  In the prototype, the base graph is the square of a path and its proper three-color phase is recovered in linear time.  Likewise, the triage proposal "plant a partition, then add compatible planar edges" has no theorem in the paper showing that its generated distribution is hard, and the plant may be statistically visible.  Declaring either construction Track A would therefore confuse worst-case NP-completeness with distributional hardness.
-
-## Why Track B also fails
-
-The rendered statement names a noncollinear anchor triangle.  Any reader can enforce immediately that its three colors are a permutation of `0,1,2`.  Interpolating those six permutations gives the **entire structure-aware candidate space**; it is not the `1,027,242,720` syntactic coefficient triples originally reported.
-
-At the shipping preset `n=2400, q=1009`, exactly **2 of those 6 rules verify**.  Corrected sampling found **66,449 hits in 200,000 attempts**, or `0.332245`, versus the required probability below `1e-6`.  The corrected random-restart attack solved **8/8** instances, normally after only a few candidates.
-
-The relevant costs make the failure explicit:
-
-| route | measured cost at `n=2400, q=1009` |
-|---|---:|
-| structure-aware enumeration | 6 candidates; 2 valid |
-| one modular interpolation | 60 counted exact operations |
-| all six interpolations | 360 counted operations, before testing another vertex |
-| seeded random-candidate baseline | 2 candidates, 0.001521 s |
-| full reference algorithm including expansion and exhaustive verification | 110,443--110,587 operations; 0.042105 s for the recorded shipping instance |
-
-The nominal 110k-versus-198 gap was spurious.  The 110k figure mostly counts re-expanding and rechecking the 21,600-vertex target after the answer has already been obtained.  Certificate production by the "mechanical" and "compact" routes is the same six-interpolation procedure.  Its actual counted compact cost is at least 360, already over G9(c)'s 300-operation cap, and its answer space is one-in-three guessable.  There is consequently no compression gap here that tests insight rather than routine execution.
-
-## Gate outcome
-
-The corrected retained module records:
-
-- G1--G3, G7, and G8: pass;
-- G4: fail (`66,449/200,000`, structure-aware space 6);
-- G5: fail (shipping density `0.332245`);
-- G6: fail (structure-aware random restart `8/8`);
-- G9(c): fail (360 intended operations); G9(b) was not reached.
-
-The hardening transcripts contain only OpenRouter HTTP 403 total-key-limit errors.  Those errors consumed zero attempts and are **not** evidence of model failure; the rejection rests on the deterministic local gates above, not on the unavailable oracle service.
-
-The failed implementation is retained as `rejected_gen_2602_13037.py` so the decision can be audited or revisited.
