index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..dd3238f992aca801d06acb5a1a4cc0c287865960
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,105 @@
+# arXiv 2602.13037 — recursive-cube mixed coloring
+
+**Status:** locally verified candidate, **not yet shippable**. G1–G8 and the G9 size/effort caps pass. The required bare, structural-hint, and placebo oracle runs completed no attempts because OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “total key limit exceeded” on every call. Those errors are preserved in the official transcripts and are not counted as failures by a model.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — no-tool compression |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite discrete |
+| computational core | graph |
+| certificate form | integer tuple / ordered set system |
+| native objects | bipartite planar graph, mixed distance coloring, cube two-pole decomposition |
+| intuition | decomposition |
+| domain essentiality | native |
+| reduction | none |
+
+## Problem and trust model
+
+This generator covers the paper’s native `(1^1,2^3)`-coloring problem from Section 2.2 of [*Between proper and square coloring of planar graphs, hardness and extremal graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13037). Such a coloring has one color that cannot repeat across an edge and three colors that cannot repeat at graph distance one or two.
+
+The displayed graph is the full subdivision of a cubic bipartite planar core. The core begins as the cube `Q_3`; each construction step replaces one edge by a fresh cube with one edge removed. The requested witness is the leaf-to-root list of eight-vertex patches. Generation carries this list through a random relabeling, so it never solves its output. Verification suppresses degree-two vertices, checks and collapses every claimed cube two-pole, explicitly reconstructs a three-edge-coloring, converts it to the mixed vertex coloring, and scans every edge and length-two path. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+Theorem 6 proves NP-completeness for bipartite planar graphs of maximum degree four and arbitrary prescribed girth. That is context, not an average-case claim for this maximum-degree-three, cube-rich distribution. This is therefore honestly Track B. The disclosed reference algorithm repeatedly finds two-edge cuts with bridge DFS and recognizes cubes in `O(r m (n+m))` exact graph operations. At `hard` it used 146,400 counted operations and 0.054 seconds for one instance; the eight-seed panel took 1.291 seconds and solved 8/8 as expected. Once the nested decomposition is recognized, the intended route is at most 264 local adjacency operations. Section 2 also identifies easy boundaries—`(1^1,2^1)` star forests and `(1^0,2^3)` unions of paths and cycles—which this family avoids.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=0, seed=0)`, `render` emits the following complete hand-scale instance:
+
+```text
+Mixed distance-(1,2) coloring certificate
+
+The undirected simple graph below has vertices 0 through 19.
+An edge u-v is unordered.  There are 24 edges:
+  0-8 3-18 2-13 14-15 7-18 6-16 3-10 5-10
+  1-11 7-14 4-18 0-1 13-17 2-12 0-5 6-11
+  14-19 11-12 8-17 2-15 16-19 9-10 9-16 4-17
+
+A (1^1,2^3)-coloring uses one distance-1 color and three distance-2
+colors. Two adjacent vertices may not share the distance-1 color. Two
+distinct vertices at graph distance 1 or 2 may not share a distance-2 color.
+
+Form the degree-three core by suppressing every degree-two vertex. Give
+exactly 0 ordered rows of eight vertices. After all rows are peeled, the
+remaining eight core vertices must induce the cube Q_3.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one JSON object with
+the single key "patches". Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>{"patches":[]}</answer>`. Suppressing the twelve degree-two vertices exposes a cube, so this is genuinely checkable by hand. `verify(inst, {"patches": []})` returns `(True, "ok")`; `verify(inst, {})` returns `(False, "answer_shape: expected one JSON key named patches")`.
+
+## Difficulty and measured gates
+
+| preset | patches `n` | displayed vertices | answer atoms | ships? |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 0 | 20 | 0 | no; hand example |
+| easy | 4 | 100 | 32 | no |
+| medium | 7 | 160 | 56 | no |
+| hard | 10 | 220 | 80 | candidate shipping preset |
+
+| gate | result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 planted verifies | pass, 12/12 across all presets |
+| G2 corruption | pass, five corruptions and five distinct reason codes |
+| G3 round trip | pass, tagged JSON recovered from surrounding prose/fence |
+| G4 structured guessing | pass, 0/200,000; candidate space `405114246492469383880569411508727111186984982456058912421243354102184620671500000000` |
+| G5 density and baseline | pass, shipping estimate 0/200,000; demo exact valid count 1; decoder 146,400 operations |
+| G6 adversaries | pass, four attacks 0/8 each; reference decoder 8/8 |
+| G7 scaling | pass, `n=20` builds and verifies (420 vertices) |
+| G8 canonical key | pass, 20/20 relabelings invariant, 20/20 carried witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | **pending/fail**, caps pass but no completed oracle attempt |
+
+The four failing attacks are degree-core plus low IDs, nearest-core greedy grouping, 256 structured random restarts, and a one-pass static cube scan. The last is the plausible in-context Track-B attack: it sees an exposed end but does not recompute the graph after each collapse.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9
+
+| run | preset | completed attempts | recorded calls | result |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| structural hint | hard | 0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+
+The individual seeds and vendor names are in `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl`, `g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl`, and `g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl`. With zero completed attempts, hinted-minus-placebo is recorded as `0.0` only as a placeholder and supports no conclusion. The hard answer measures 317 characters, about 80 tokens, and 80 atomic elements; the intended route is 264 exact operations. All are under the G9(c) caps.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2602_13037 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(n=10, seed=7)
+statement = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer('<answer>{"patches": []}</answer>')
+ok, reason = g.verify(inst, answer)  # False here: the hard instance needs 10 rows
+ok, reason = g.verify(inst, inst["answer"])  # True, "ok"
+```
+
+From the repository root, emission—only after G9 has a real hardened verdict—is:
+
+```bash
+scripts/emit.sh results/2602.13037/gen_2602_13037.py
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This family is easy with graph tooling: the polynomial reference decoder is deliberate and fully reported. The paper’s NP-completeness theorem does not establish hardness for this generated distribution, whose short cycles violate the theorem’s large-girth flavor. The `0/200,000` estimate concerns uniform ordered partitions into disjoint eight-vertex core sets after enforcing type, size, degree, order, disjointness, and residue size; it is not a bound against topology-aware search. No general graph-isomorphism canonicalizer, SAT/SMT solver, or tree-decomposition dynamic program was run. The canonical key is a strong distance-profile invariant, not a complete isomorphism invariant for arbitrary graphs. Finally, the archived `rejected_gen_2602_13037.py` is a different affine prototype that correctly failed G4 and G6; it is retained as audit evidence and is not the shipping module.
